in previous session we learned RBAC (ROLE BASED ACCESS CONTROL)

why we used this is RBAC (ROLE BASED ACCESS CONTROL) for assigining a permissions like for specific person only giving specific permissions

now we will learn about RESOURCE QUOTA

There are two types of restrictions that need to be mentioned while using ResourceQuota

1. LIMIT: Represents the maximum amount of CPU or memory the pod can use.

2. REQUEST: Represents the minimum amount of CPU or memory that the pod is guaranteed to have.

lets do in practical way

kubectl create ns dev -------------> command to create namespace with name dev

now we need to allocate resources to that created namespace

lets write resource-quota manifest file

note for to know cpu:
=======================

1 cpu = 1 core
1 cpu = 1000m
1/2 cpu = 500m
1/4 cpu = 250m

Mi --------> means mega byte
m ---------> memory

vim resource-quota.yml

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: dev-rq
spec:
  hard:
    limits.cpu: "400m"
    request.cpu: "200m"
    limits.memory: "100Mi"
    requests.memory: "50Mi"

:wq


before going to execute will see namespace once

kubectl describe ns dev ---------> command to view full information of created namespace with name dev

kubectl create -f resource-quota.yml ------------> command to execute above manifest file

lets create pod 

vim pod.yml

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
  namespace: dev
spec:
containers:
  - name: cont1
    image: nginx
    ports:
      - containerPort: 80
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "5Mi"
        cpu: "50m"
      limits:
        memory: "10Mi"
        cpu: "100m"

:wq

kubectl create -f pod.yml ----------------> command to execute above manifest file

need to see the details of pod

kubectl describe pod mypod-n dev ------------> command to view details of pod


